+# GOAL — block 26: the unsoldered formation law in level time — gain-one spin waves, the local-limit constant, finite planes forget, and the simulated algebraic decay of the initial plane's memory (2026-09-16)
+
+**Directive.** Owner 2026-09-16: "keep running the campaign for 12 more hours." The lane's proof-shaped queue was exhausted after block 25's sharpening (the arrow budget refuted; the band, the normalization and X4 hard). Stepping back: the campaign has the static side of the unsoldered (sphere) reading — block 19 (order on `Z³` for `β > 76/100`, blocks 21–23) — and the formation side of the six-axis reading — block 25 (order at strong coupling). The formation side of the sphere reading is unworked: block 13 (T5b) linearized it and found gain one with variance `1/(3β)`, and explicitly left "whether an aligned region exists at all" open. Exploration this morning: the sphere formation law started from an aligned plane loses its magnetization at every coupling tested (`β = 3, 6, 12`) with no plateau over `3000` levels, at rates that track the gain-one spin-wave prediction at large `β`; the six-axis formation law at the same `β` (weights `e^{β}, e^{−β}, 1`) keeps its plane. This is the qualitative discriminator the campaign lacked: under the sphere menu the reading decides memory; under the six-axis menu both readings keep it.
+
+**Exact target (claim type `bounded_theorem`; trace class `frontier_discovery`).** For the unsoldered formation law in level order on `Z³` — the record at `x` drawn from `K_β(s | s_{x−e_1}, s_{x−e_2}, s_{x−e_3}) ∝ e^{β s·S}`, `S = Σ_j s_{x−e_j}`, read as the level automaton on `Z²` — prove exactly:
+- T1 (the kernel): `K_β(·|S)` is the exponential-overlap law with concentration `κ = β|S|` about `û = S/|S|`; `E[s | S] = A(κ)û`, `E[s sᵀ | S] = (A/κ) I + (1 − 3A/κ) ûûᵀ` with `A(κ) = coth κ − 1/κ`; `A(κ) < κ/3` for `κ > 0`; hence the one-step magnetization from the aligned plane is `A(3β)` and the transverse second moment of one step at the aligned plane is `2A(3β)/(3β)`.
+- T2 (gain one, re-proved for the sphere; block 13 is a sibling, not a premise): rotation covariance forces the linearized transverse mean to be the average of the three predecessors' transverse coordinates; the exact one-step transverse variance per component is `A(3β)/(3β)`.
+- T3 (the level walk's constant): `P_k = Σ_y p_k(y)²` (the k-step return sum of the three-predecessor walk, `p_k` trinomial) satisfies `3√3/(4πk) − 1/k² − (5/(2k))e^{−k/4} ≤ P_k ≤ 3√3/(4πk) + 27/k² + e^{−√k/7}`; hence `k P_k → 3√3/(4π)` and `Σ_{k≤t} P_k ≥ (3√3/(4π)) H_t − 3`.
+- T4 (finite planes forget): on every periodic `L×L` level plane the automaton has a unique invariant law, invariant under all rotations, so the magnetization from any initial plane tends to `0`; for `L = 1` exactly `m_t = A(3β)^t`.
+- T5 (the linearized field): the transverse field of the linearization has variance `v_t = (A(3β)/(3β)) Σ_{k<t} P_k` per component, `v_t = γ(β) log t + O(1)` with `γ(β) = (3√3/(4π))·A(3β)/(3β)`; no stationary law of the linearized field; the linearized magnetization decays as `t^{−γ(β)}`.
+- Executed, not proved (frontier discovery): the nonlinear law simulated on periodic planes up to `512×512` for `20000` levels at `β = 3, 6, 12, 24`: the magnetization decays with no plateau, with local exponents approaching `γ(β)` from above as `β` grows; the six-axis formation law at weights `(e^{β}, e^{−β}, 1)` keeps its plane at the same `β`; finite-size series; an independent sampler; the linear model against its exact variance.
+
+**Falsifiers.** A kernel moment differing from `A(κ)`, `1 − 2A/κ`; `A(κ) ≥ κ/3` somewhere; a linearized mean other than the average; a `P_k` outside the two-sided bounds; a path count differing from the trinomial identity; `m_t ≠ A(3β)^t` on the one-site plane; a simulated magnetization that settles at a positive plateau on the infinite-plane scale (`t ≪ L²`) at some `β` — this would refute the discovery claim, not the theorems.
+
+**Lens pass (self-run panel).**
+- *"Block 13 already has gain one and `1/(3β)`."* Yes; T2 re-proves it at scope in one paragraph because the note must be self-contained against `main`; the new exact content is T1's nonlinear identities, T3's constant with explicit error bounds (block 13 had only `1/(36n) ≤ P_n ≤ 9π/(16n)`), T4, and T5's exponent. The new science is the executed nonlinear decay and the contrast with blocks 19 and 25.
+- *"A simulation is not a theorem."* Stated as such everywhere: claim type carries the exact parts; the decay on the infinite plane is a frontier discovery with the falsifier named; no sentence says the sphere formation law "has no ordered phase".
+- *"Finite planes forget trivially."* Yes (T4 is elementary); its value is the exact `L = 1` rate and the statement that the memory time's growth with `L` is the whole question — polynomial (diffusive, `L²` in the linear theory) against the six-axis law's contours.
+- *"Why not the twist argument (a two-dimensional symmetry argument in level time)?"* Tried at the whiteboard: an in-plane twist costs an amount proportional to the number of levels (the fluctuation-generated in-plane cost is of order one per site per level), so the box argument gives nothing; recorded under N1.
+- *"Names."* The exponential-overlap law on the sphere and `A(κ)` are named after their authors only under Prior art, Premises and Imports; theorems use `K_β` and `A(κ)`.
+
+**Forbidden phrases (beyond the lane's standing list):** "has no ordered phase", "does not order", "Mermin", "Wagner", "spontaneous symmetry breaking", "quasi-long-range", "converge", "critical", "certified".
+
+**Prior-art search at `origin/main`.** Nothing on the sphere formation law in level time; the static sphere law (blocks 19–23) and the six-axis formation law (blocks 08–12, 25) are open PRs, referenced as evidence addresses; block 01 (main) supplies the rule.
